<article class="env-lemma" id="09XG">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/09XG">Lemma <span data-tag="09XG">15.9.9</span>.</a> Let $I$ be an ideal of a ring $A$. Let $A \to B$ be an integral ring map. Let $b \in B$ map to an idempotent in $B/IB$. Then there exists a monic $f \in A[x]$ with $f(b) = 0$ and $f \bmod I = x^ d(x - 1)^ d$ for some $d \geq 1$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Observe that $z = b^2 - b$ is an element of $IB$. By Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/00H5" data-tag="00H5">10.38.4</a> there exist a monic polynomial $g(x) = x^ d + \sum a_ j x^ j$ of degree $d$ with $a_ j \in I$ such that $g(z) = 0$ in $B$. Hence $f(x) = g(x^2 - x) \in A[x]$ is a monic polynomial such that $f(x) \equiv x^ d(x - 1)^ d \bmod I$ and such that $f(b) = 0$ in $B$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>